From 556d5ea46a83567e36bafb5adccffad5eb16ebed Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Newton <rrnewton@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 11:17:41 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Emit order-independent guest env hash in DETLOG for
 cross-backend diffing

Add a one-shot DETLOG line at exec that summarizes the guest's environment,
lazily computed only when INFO logging is active so a normal run pays nothing:

    DETLOG [env, dtid N] count=163 hash=<sha256> keys_hash=<sha256> keys=A,B,...

The environment is read from the guest's initial process stack through
`guest.memory()`, which behaves identically across backends (ptrace/KVM/DBI);
reading /proc/<pid>/environ would be wrong under KVM, where guest.pid() is the
host VMM. Entries are sorted before hashing, so the digest is order-independent
and a backend that enumerates `environ` in a different order does not
manufacture a spurious difference.

Two hashes plus a name list make environment divergence a one-line comparison:
- `hash` covers the sorted VAR=VALUE set and moves on any value change;
- `keys_hash` covers just the sorted variable names, so a key-set difference
  is visible even when a value change would otherwise mask it;
- `keys` is the sorted, comma-joined name list (values excluded, as they may
  be secrets or host-specific paths), answering "which vars differ".

This directly serves flaky-failure attribution: a single line distinguishes
"the environments differed" (and in which keys) from Hermit nondeterminism,
and answers "did ptrace and KVM see the same environment?".

Add a hermetic cli regression test (env cleared then controlled) proving:
default runs emit no line (zero-cost), identical envs hash identically,
reordered envs hash identically (order-independence), a value-only change
moves `hash` but not `keys_hash`/`keys`, and adding a key moves all three.
